Below is a minimal Java‑style snippet that shows how to use the **Res9patchStreamDecoder.decode** helper to turn a 9‑patch image (read from an `InputStream`) into a normal PNG image (written to an `OutputStream`).  
The snippet assumes you already have the two streams (`imageInputStream` and `imageOutputStream`) available in your code.

```java
import java.io.InputStream;
import java.io.OutputStream;

// ... your other imports ...

public class NinePatchDecoder {

    /**
     * Decodes a 9‑patch image from the given InputStream and writes the resulting PNG
     * image to the supplied OutputStream.
     *
     * @param imageInputStream  the InputStream containing the 9‑patch image data
     * @param imageOutputStream the OutputStream where the decoded PNG image will be written
     * @throws IOException if an I/O error occurs during decoding or writing
     */
    public static void decodeNinePatch(InputStream imageInputStream,
                                       OutputStream imageOutputStream) throws IOException {
        // Call the helper function provided by the platform
        boolean success = Res9patchStreamDecoder.decode(imageInputStream, imageOutputStream);

        if (!success) {
            throw new IOException("Failed to decode the 9‑patch image.");
        }
    }

    // Example usage
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        try (InputStream imageInputStream = /* obtain your 9‑patch InputStream */;
             OutputStream imageOutputStream = /* obtain your desired OutputStream */) {

            decodeNinePatch(imageInputStream, imageOutputStream);
            System.out.println("Decoding completed successfully.");

        } catch (IOException e) {
            e.printStackTrace();
        }
    }
